.TH PDEHOCTEST 1
.SH NAME
pdehoctest \- test "equation hoc" against a fixed equation it can express
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pdehoctest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Pdehoctest
checks
.IR pde (2)'s
.B "equation hoc"
\(em a caller writes the update rule itself, as
.IR hoc (2)
expression strings, instead of supplying numbers into one of the three
equations this module has fixed Limbo code for.
There is no independent oracle for an arbitrary user equation, so this
test uses the one equation both paths can express: Gray-Scott
reaction-diffusion, once as
.B "equation gray"
(the fixed function) and once as
.B "equation hoc"
with the identical formula written out as expression strings, run from
the same seed for the same number of steps.
Any real difference between the two would mean
.B "equation hoc"
is not doing what it claims.
.PP
The two problems agree on every cell exactly, not merely to a
tolerance \(em both do the same arithmetic in the same order, so there
is no rounding path for them to disagree along.
.PP
This is a comparison, and a comparison that always agrees has not been
shown to compare anything.
.B Gray-Scott's
own reaction term clamps every substep, so a second run deliberately
perturbs one hoc expression (feed by one percent) and confirms the
result now differs from
.B "equation gray"
by something well above rounding \(em otherwise the exact-match check
above would have no teeth.
.PP
The mesh line in both specs states an explicit
.B domain
matching the grid size
.RI ( "domain NxN" "" for an " NxN " mesh).
.IR Mesh (2)'s
own default domain is the unit square, which makes
.I dx
a small fraction of a cell rather than 1 \(em di\%viding a
discrete Laplacian by that small
.IR dx \(mu dx
inflates it by roughly the square of the grid size.
That is not a bug in the stencil or in
.BR evalarray() ;
it found this test's own first spec, which left the domain unstated and
so ran the identical reaction-diffusion formula at a diffusivity
effectively thousands of times too strong for four substeps to remain
stable at.
.B "Equation gray's"
own
.RI [0,1]
clamp absorbed the resulting blow-up every substep and still produced a
plausible-looking field; the hoc side, with no clamp of its own (see
.IR pde (2)
\(em hoc has no clamp/min/max builtin), carried it forward unclamped
into
.BR Infinity ,
then
.BR NaN .
Both sides were doing the same wrong arithmetic; only one side's
symptom was visible.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/pdehoctest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR hoc (1),
.IR pde (2),
.IR hoc (2),
.IR pdetest (1)
.SH BUGS
Checks one equation (Gray-Scott) and one grid size.
The general claim \(em that
.I any
hoc expression referencing a field's own name, its
.BR lap_ NAME
Laplacian, and declared
.B param
names works \(em rests on this one case plus
.IR hoc (2)'s
own much larger regression suite for the expression language itself,
not on a second independent equation.
.PP
Only
.BR step() 's
explicit path is exercised.
.B "Equation hoc"
has no implicit solver (matching
.BR gray ),
so
.I gmres
/
.I cg
are never reached here.
.PP
This
.I session's
own
.B nantest
probe (not part of the tree) separately found that this Dis build's
real
.BR > / < ""
do not follow IEEE 754 for
.B NaN
operands \(em
.RI ( "NaN > 0.0" ,
.RI "NaN < 0.0" ", and even" " 0.0 > NaN"
all evaluated true) \(em which is why the runaway diffusivity above
first showed up as a worst-difference of
.B NaN
comparing greater than every real
.IR worst ,
not as a comparison that silently passed.
Not chased further here; a real, independent finding left for whoever
next needs Dis-level real-comparison correctness against
.BR NaN .
